WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.421
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:14.682
Unit twenty-one: Grant Writing. Today you will make careful, credible claims using hedging.

4
00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:17.553
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.733
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:23.375
Grant.

7
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:27.718
Grant means a sum of money awarded to fund a specific project.

8
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:31.036
The team secured a three-year research grant.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.896
Your turn. Say: Grant.

10
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:37.444
Grant.

11
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:39.138
Proposal.

12
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.856
Proposal means a formal document requesting funding for planned work.

13
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.926
The proposal must not exceed ten pages.

14
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:50.076
Your turn. Say: Proposal.

15
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:53.969
Proposal.

16
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:55.404
Funding.

17
00:00:56.417 --> 00:00:59.554
Funding means financial support provided for a purpose.

18
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.684
Competition for funding is intense.

19
00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:05.344
Your turn. Say: Funding.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:08.992
Funding.

21
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:10.937
Objectives.

22
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.283
Objectives means specific, measurable aims a project intends to achieve.

23
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.697
Each objective should be measurable.

24
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:21.994
Your turn. Say: Objectives.

25
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:25.985
Objectives.

26
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:27.589
Budget.

27
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:31.790
Budget means a detailed estimate of planned expenditure.

28
00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:34.662
Reviewers questioned the travel budget.

29
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.461
Your turn. Say: Budget.

30
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.139
Budget.

31
00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:42.803
Impact.

32
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:47.558
Impact means the measurable effect of research beyond academia.

33
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.415
Funders increasingly prioritise social impact.

34
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:54.367
Your turn. Say: Impact.

35
00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:58.339
Impact.

36
00:01:59.083 --> 00:01:59.829
Reviewer.

37
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:04.651
Reviewer means an expert who assesses proposals for funders.

38
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.074
Reviewers score each proposal out of one hundred.

39
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:11.429
Your turn. Say: Reviewer.

40
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.337
Reviewer.

41
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:17.131
Feasibility.

42
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:22.218
Feasibility means the likelihood that a plan can be successfully carried out.

43
00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:25.397
A pilot study demonstrated feasibility.

44
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:28.914
Your turn. Say: Feasibility.

45
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:32.872
Feasibility.

46
00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:37.445
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:44.883
Visal, your draft grant proposal says the project 'will eliminate illiteracy in rural schools'. Is that wise?

48
00:02:45.983 --> 00:02:54.896
Probably not. It sounds overconfident. I've rewritten it: the project is likely to have a significant impact on rural literacy.

49
00:02:55.983 --> 00:02:58.320
Doesn't hedging make you sound uncertain?

50
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:09.407
Not if it's done carefully. Reviewers may distrust exaggerated claims, whereas cautious ones suggest the team understands the risks.

51
00:03:10.517 --> 00:03:12.853
How else can you persuade reviewers?

52
00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:21.409
By demonstrating feasibility. Our pilot results could indicate that the approach works on a larger scale.

53
00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:23.916
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.027
Grammar focus: Hedging (likely to, may, could).

55
00:03:28.327 --> 00:03:29.537
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.283
The project is likely to have a significant impact.

57
00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:36.938
Reviewers may distrust exaggerated claims.

58
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.092
Our pilot results could indicate that the approach works.

59
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:53.106
Hedging softens claims to match the strength of your evidence. Combine devices for precision: 'is likely to', 'appears to', 'may potentially'. Avoid over-hedging, which sounds evasive.

60
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.081
Be careful! Don't say: The project is likely having an impact.

61
00:04:00.033 --> 00:04:03.205
Say: The project is likely to have an impact.

62
00:04:03.505 --> 00:04:05.528
Use 'likely to' + base verb.

63
00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:08.870
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:10.854
Winning the Funding.

65
00:04:11.154 --> 00:04:16.056
Grant writing is a persuasive genre in which researchers compete for limited resources.

66
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:22.747
A strong proposal sets out clear objectives, a realistic budget and a convincing case for impact.

67
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:30.981
Experienced writers avoid absolute promises, preferring claims such as 'the project is likely to have a significant impact'.

68
00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:37.194
Such hedging may appear modest, yet it signals that the applicants understand the uncertainties involved.

69
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:44.530
Above all, reviewers want evidence of feasibility, since even brilliant ideas are worthless if they cannot be delivered.

70
00:04:45.633 --> 00:04:49.960
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.817 --> 00:04:53.815
The project is likely blank a significant impact.

72
00:04:54.115 --> 00:04:57.211
A, having. B, to have. C, have.

73
00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.592
The answer is B: to have.

74
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.691
Which claim is appropriately hedged?

75
00:05:05.991 --> 00:05:13.557
A, This will solve poverty.. B, This may contribute to reducing poverty.. C, This perhaps possibly might maybe help..

76
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.305
The answer is B: This may contribute to reducing poverty..

77
00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.854
The likelihood that a plan can be carried out is its blank.

78
00:05:26.154 --> 00:05:29.819
A, impact. B, feasibility. C, budget.

79
00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:35.970
The answer is B: feasibility.

80
00:05:37.117 --> 00:05:39.348
Over-hedging makes a writer sound blank.

81
00:05:39.648 --> 00:05:43.430
A, evasive. B, confident. C, precise.

82
00:05:46.967 --> 00:05:48.966
The answer is A: evasive.

83
00:05:50.117 --> 00:05:53.358
According to the reading, why do experienced writers hedge?

84
00:05:53.658 --> 00:06:00.818
A, to signal they understand uncertainty. B, to make proposals shorter. C, because reviewers prefer vague claims.

85
00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:08.015
The answer is A: to signal they understand uncertainty.

86
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.479
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:16.675
What makes a research proposal convincing to reviewers?

88
00:06:33.483 --> 00:06:34.926
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.226 --> 00:07:11.590
A convincing proposal is likely to combine a clear problem, realistic objectives and strong evidence of feasibility. Reviewers tend to trust applicants who acknowledge risks rather than promising dramatic results. For example, pilot data showing that a reading programme works in two schools may persuade them that it could work in twenty. Some applicants believe ambitious claims attract attention, and occasionally they do. In most cases, however, careful, honest claims are more persuasive.

90
00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.738
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:19.160
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging (likely to, may, could).

92
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.307
Today's value is planning ahead. Make a plan and take small steps every day.

93
00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:27.329
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:30.989
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

